faq_data=[
('ING-FAQ-01','Which heavy metals have been measured in cocoa?', 'Cadmium and lead were measured across the FDA cocoa-and-chocolate sample, nickel was measured in Ecuadorian cocoa beans, and aluminium was measured in German cocoa powder; these are different sampling frames rather than a guarantee that every sample contains the same amounts.',[A,R,F]),
('ING-FAQ-02','Why does cocoa contain cadmium?', 'Soil uptake matters: the cacao synthesis identified total soil cadmium and pH as important bean-cadmium predictors, with substantial variation that a single soil measurement could not fully explain.',[W]),
('ING-FAQ-03','Is organic cocoa free from heavy metals?', 'No such conclusion follows from an organic label: the selected US survey did not find consistently lower lead, cadmium or arsenic in organic-labelled cocoa and dark-chocolate products, and its observational comparison does not prove that organic production caused the differences.',[H]),
('ING-FAQ-04','Does a lower cocoa percentage mean less cadmium?', 'Cocoa percentage was positively associated with cadmium in the FDA retail sample, but origin and formulation also varied, so the association does not guarantee a lower result for every lower-percentage product.',[A]),
('ING-FAQ-05','Which growing origins had lower measured cadmium?', 'The FDA African-origin products were lower than Latin American products, while the primary dissertation’s twelve Côte d’Ivoire whole-bean samples had a mean of 0.038 mg/kg versus 1.153 mg/kg in four Ecuadorian samples; these historical, unmatched source groups do not guarantee a future lot.',[A,OT]),
('ING-FAQ-06','Can chocolate and cocoa drinks also contain these metals?', 'Yes: the FDA survey measured cadmium and lead in cocoa powder, nibs and chocolate products, but the amount of powder, liquid and other ingredients must be known before a powder result can describe a prepared drink.',[A]),
('ING-FAQ-07','Does a concentration result tell me my health risk?', 'It supplies one input: intake also depends on portion size, frequency, body weight and the rest of the diet, as illustrated by the German cocoa exposure model rather than established by a concentration alone.',[F]),
('ING-FAQ-08','Are cocoa powder and cocoa beans subject to the same legal limits?', 'Do not assume so: the EU cadmium entry at 0.60 mg/kg and nickel entry at 15 mg/kg identify specified consumer cocoa-powder categories, while chocolate has separate entries; the named powder values are not universal raw-bean ceilings.',[EU,EN]),
('ING-FAQ-09','Can processing remove cadmium from cocoa?', 'Processing can redistribute it: the bean-processing study found a tendency for cadmium to remain in non-lipid fractions, so a lower result in cocoa butter is not proof that the original metal mass was destroyed or that powder also became lower.',[O]),
('ING-FAQ-11','Is cocoa butter equivalent to cocoa powder for metal testing?', 'No: the processing evidence distinguishes a lipid fraction from non-lipid fractions with different contaminant partitioning, so testing cocoa butter does not characterize the powder from the same operation.',[O]),
('ING-FAQ-13','Can the German exposure estimates be used for young children?', 'The model’s consumption data came from participants aged 14–80, so it does not establish exposure or a suitable serving for infants and young children.',[F]),
('ING-FAQ-16','How much cocoa is safe to eat each week?', 'This evidence does not establish one weekly amount for every product and person: the German model combines concentration and consumption and cannot turn a historical group mean into an individual serving guarantee.',[F]),
('COCOA-FAQ-AL','Which cocoa powder origins had lower aluminium?', 'In the German historical table, Bolivia’s single labelled sample measured 5 mg/kg and the four-sample American-origin group averaged 45 mg/kg, versus 151 mg/kg in seven African-origin samples; the small groups and imperfect labels prevent a dependable regional preference.',[F]),
('COCOA-FAQ-NI','Does a low cadmium test also mean low nickel or lead?', 'Separate measurements are needed: the dissertation’s lowest reported cadmium mean was in Côte d’Ivoire cocoa beans, while its lowest reported lead mean was in Ecuadorian cocoa beans, with censored lead results; the separate nine-province study also did not establish a linear relationship among cadmium, lead and nickel.',[OT,R]),
('COCOA-FAQ-ORIGIN','Does the packing country identify where cacao was grown?', 'Not reliably: the German study found many declarations referred to European processing or packing locations, while only twelve powder samples had plausible growing-origin information.',[F]),
('COCOA-FAQ-TEST','What does a lead result below detection mean?', 'It is a result bounded by the method’s detection limit, as retained in the FDA lead range, rather than a measured zero or proof that lead is absent.',[A]),
('COCOA-FAQ-SENS','Why is nickel relevant to people who are already sensitised?', 'EFSA’s acute oral assessment identified eczematous skin flare-ups in nickel-sensitised people as the critical effect, so general population exposure averages do not answer every sensitised person’s question.',[N])]
